 The Brain Rule for Laravel Projects
Core Principle
The /brain folder acts as the central intelligence of your Laravel system. It captures everything — from architecture decisions and test results to feature plans and deployment checklists. This guarantees that even months or years later, any developer (or AI agent) can read the /brain folder and fully understand what was done, how, why, and under what reasoning.

The /brain fulfills the same role as product management tools, documentation systems, and quality assurance combined — but stored natively inside your repository.

📁 Folder Structure
text
/brain
  /features
    /[FeatureName]
      - README.md
      - journal.md
      - tests.md
      - bugs.md
      - decisions.md
      - migrations.md
  /architecture
      - system-overview.md
      - folder-structure.md
      - service-providers.md
      - database-relationships.md
      - ADRs/ (Architecture Decision Records)
  /qa
      - testing-strategy.md
      - test-reports.md
      - performance-metrics.md
  /security
      - threat-models.md
      - authentication.md
      - encryption-policies.md
      - audit-log.md
  /operations
      - deployment-instructions.md
      - environment-setup.md
      - CI-CD-config.md
      - rollback-plan.md
  /project-memory
      - timeline.md
      - changelog.md
      - state-of-project.md
      - rejected-ideas.md
      - roadmap.md
🧩 Feature Folder Standard
Each feature in Laravel (for instance, UserRegistration, PaymentGateway, AdminDashboard) has its own subfolder under /brain/features.
Every subfolder includes five documents:

README.md – High-level goal, routes, user story, and module ownership.

journal.md – Daily or sprint-based notes on progress, blockers, changes.

tests.md – PHPUnit coverage results, failed tests, test strategy.

bugs.md – Active vs resolved issues, severity, replication steps.

decisions.md – Architecture choices, alternatives considered, and ADR references.

Optional:

migrations.md – Documentation of all DB migrations related to that feature.

⚙️ Laravel-Specific Integrations
1. Service Provider Integration
Every new feature documented under /brain/features also has a Service Provider registered (feature-based modularization ):​

php
php artisan make:provider ProductFeatureServiceProvider
Each provider acts as that feature’s runtime point of injection, linked in /brain/features/[FeatureName]/README.md under “Boots via:”.

2. Folder-by-Feature Convention
Instead of default type-based /app/Models, /Controllers, structure the app by feature for maintainability :​

text
app/
 ├── User/
 │    ├── UserController.php
 │    ├── UserService.php
 │    ├── UserPolicy.php
 ├── Orders/
 │    ├── OrderController.php
 │    ├── OrderService.php
 │    ├── OrderModel.php
Linked documentation for each is inside /brain/features/User and /brain/features/Orders.

🏗️ Architecture Documentation
The /architecture folder records how the Laravel application scales, interacts, and evolves over time. It tracks service providers, controllers, migrations, and configurations.

system-overview.md

Overview diagram (e.g., requests → middleware → controller → model → view)

Explanation of domain boundaries

ADRs/
Contains Architecture Decision Records following Nygard’s MADR or Tyree template :​

text
# ADR-001: Choose Laravel Queue System
**Date:** 2025-10-24  
**Status:** Accepted  
**Context:** We need async background jobs.
**Decision:** Use Laravel Horizon with Redis driver.
**Alternatives:** RabbitMQ, SQS.
**Consequences:** Simplifies worker scaling, introduces Redis dependency.
service-providers.md
Registered service providers list with their feature link.

database-relationships.md
ERD schema overview, migration dependencies, foreign key map.

🧪 Testing & QA Workflow
Laravel ships with PHPUnit and Pest. The /brain/qa folder centralizes quality tracking.

testing-strategy.md
Outlines test layers: Unit (Model), Feature (Controller + Request), Integration (API).

test-reports.md
Contains PHPUnit coverage summaries, test performance, runtime.

performance-metrics.md
Stores Blackfire or Laravel Telescope data (load times, DB query count).

QA entries follow the test-driven development cycle:

Test written ➜ 2. Code implemented ➜ 3. Documentation updated ➜ 4. Tests stored in /brain/features/[Feature]/tests.md

🔒 Security and Compliance
Laravel has built-in policies, guards, and encryption.
The /brain/security folder contains:

threat-models.md – STRIDE model for security review per feature

authentication.md – Guard configurations, Sanctum/JWT use

encryption-policies.md – Sensitive data handling (passwords, secrets)

audit-log.md – Summary of vulnerabilities scanned with Laravel Security Checker

🚀 Operations Overview
Production, staging, and testing environments each must be documented within /brain/operations.

deployment-instructions.md

Steps using Envoy or CI/CD runner

Example:

bash
ssh user@server
php artisan down
git pull origin main
composer install --no-dev
php artisan migrate --force
php artisan up
CI-CD-config.md

Documents GitHub Actions or Jenkins pipelines (test + deploy stages)

rollback-plan.md

Recovery procedures for failed database migrations or deployments.

📚 Documentation Standards (PHPDoc + Markdown)
Every PHP class must have PHPDoc annotation headers :​

php
/**
 * Handle user registration logic.
 *
 * @param  Request  $request
 * @return Response
 *
 * @throws ValidationException
 */
PHPDoc complements /brain docs — it describes how the code functions, while /brain describes why it exists.

Consistency between PHPDoc, architecture ADRs, and /brain/features ensures perfect traceability.

📆 Brain Maintenance Protocol
On every commit:

If new code introduced → update /brain/features/...

If architectural change → create new ADR

If security patch → update /brain/security/audit-log.md

If migration → note in /brain/features/[Feature]/migrations.md

Each month, Cursor generates state-of-project.md summarizing:

Feature completion ratio (✅ completed / 🔨 in progress)

Test coverage percentage

Open bugs count

Security incidents or mitigations

